.TH DOTS\-VERIFY 1 "2026-08-30" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dots\-verify \- check the health of a deployed dots configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dots\-verify
.RB [ \-\-quiet ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dots\-verify
inspects the current machine against the contents of the dots repository and
reports one pass or failure per check. It changes nothing: every check is
read-only, so it is safe to run at any time.
.PP
The repository root is derived from the location of the script itself: the
parent of the directory containing
.B verify.sh
is used, so the same copy works when invoked directly or through
.BR just (1).
The platform directory is selected from
.BR uname (1):
on
.B Darwin
it becomes
.IR macos ,
on
.B Linux
it becomes
.IR linux ,
and any
.B *BSD
name becomes
.IR bsd .
Any other value is used verbatim as the platform name.
.PP
The script is written to run under the bash 3.2 that ships with macOS
(no associative arrays; the secrets target mapping is a
.B case
statement), so it works both in a fresh curl-piped bootstrap and on an
established machine. It runs under
.B "set \-euo pipefail"
and aborts at a failing command.
.PP
Five groups of checks are run.
.SS Required tools
.IR age ,
.IR stow ,
and
.B git
must each be found on
.BR PATH .
One pass or failure line is printed per tool.
.SS Symlinks: common and Symlinks: <platform>
Every regular file in each Stow package under
.I common/
and
.IR <platform>/
must be deployed at the same path below
.RI HOME ,
with the package-relative path appended to
.RI $HOME "/" .
For each such file the script requires that the target exists, is a symlink,
and resolves to the repository file: relative link targets are converted to
absolute paths before comparison. Failure messages distinguish three cases:
the target exists but is not a symlink, the target is missing entirely, and
the symlink points somewhere other than the repository. If a package base
directory does not exist, that whole group is skipped silently.
.PP
Note the coverage: only
.I common/
and
.I <platform>/
are walked.
.BR dots\-setup (1)
additionally stows the
.I alpine/
layer under Alpine and the
.I wsl/
layer under WSL; packages in those two layers are deployed below
.RI HOME
but are not inspected by these symlink checks.
.PP
The directory walk excludes files named
.BR *~ ,
.BR #*# ,
.BR ".git*" ,
or
.BR .DS_Store .
Those four names are a pragmatic approximation of GNU Stow's default ignore
list, not a match of it. The
.B *~
and
.B #*#
entries agree with Stow \(em its built-in list ignores every name ending in
.B ~
and every
.B #...#
autosave file \(em but the other two diverge from Stow's behavior in both
directions, and one divergence bites today.
.PP
Stow's defaults ignore only the exact names
.BR .git ,
.BR .gitignore ,
and
.B .gitmodules
(along with
.BR .cvsignore ,
.BR RCS ,
.BR CVS ,
.BR .svn ,
.BR .hg ,
and
.BR _darcs ),
not
.B .git*
in general. Any other
.B .git*
name \(em
.B .gitignore_global ,
.BR .github ,
and so on \(em is deployed when a package is stowed, while the walk skips it,
so a broken or missing symlink for such a file goes unreported. This does
bite in this repository:
.IR common/git/.gitignore_global ,
exists and gets stowed to
.IR ~/.gitignore_global ,
but the
.B .git*
exclusion keeps it out of the walk, so
.B verify.sh
can never report a broken or missing
.I ~/.gitignore_global
symlink.
.B .DS_Store ,
meanwhile, is not in Stow 2.4.1's built-in list at all, so Stow would deploy
one while the walk skips it \(em also unreported, though no such file exists
in this repository's packages today.
.PP
In the other direction, a top-level
.BR "README*" ,
.BR "LICENSE*" ,
or
.B COPYING
in a package is walked here even though Stow's path-anchored default ignores
keep it from being deployed, so a missing symlink for one would be reported
although Stow never created it. None of those exist in this repository's
packages either.
.SS "File permissions"
The
.I ~/.ssh
directory must have mode 700, and every regular file directly inside it whose
name matches
.B id_*
but not
.B *.pub
must have mode 600. If
.I ~/.ssh
does not exist, a single failure is reported. Modes are read with GNU
.B "stat \-c %a"
and fall back to the BSD/macOS form
.B "stat \-f %Lp" .
.SS "Decrypted secrets"
Every
.B *.age
file under
.I secrets/
must have its plaintext counterpart present. Passphrase fallback copies named
.B *.phrase.age
are skipped: they exist only as vault copies of the corresponding
.B .age
file and have no deployed target. The first path component below
.I secrets/
selects the target directory:
.B ssh
maps to
.I ~/.ssh
and
.B tokens
maps to
.IR ~/.config/tokens ;
the
.B .age
suffix is stripped from the filename, and the resulting path must exist as a
regular file in the target directory. An unmapped subdirectory is reported
as a failure, "No target mapping for\ ...".
.PP
If the walk yields no secrets to check, a note reading
.B "(no .age files found)"
is printed. The trigger is a counter of non-passphrase
.B .age
files that is still zero when the walk ends \-\-
.B *.phrase.age
copies are skipped before it is incremented \-\- so the note appears both
when
.I secrets/
is missing or holds no
.B .age
files at all and when the tree holds nothing but
.B *.phrase.age
fallback copies. The wording is a little loose in the second case
(\fB.age\fP files do exist there), but the note still reads
.BR "(no .age files found)" .
It is informational and does not count as a failure.
.SS "direnv + runtimes"
Seven checks covering the direnv + runtimes integration (see
.IR docs/direnv\-runtimes.md ):
.TP
.B direnv
on
.B PATH
\(em soft:
.BR dots\-setup (1)
installs it as a hard requirement, but
.B verify.sh
can legitimately run pre-bootstrap, so absence is reported
.RB ( "direnv not found (soft check; setup.sh installs it)" ),
not failed.
.TP
The direnv hook in
.IR ~/.bashrc
\(em hard: a missing
.IR ~/.bashrc ,
a missing hook line
.RB ( "direnv hook missing from \(ha" ),
or a missing trueline source
.RB ( "trueline source missing from \(ha (cannot confirm hook order)" )
fails. The hook must sit on a later line than the trueline source \(em
trueline replaces
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
without chaining, silently discarding a hook placed before it \(em so a
hook at or above the trueline line also fails, with both line numbers in
the message.
.TP
The repository's
.I common/direnv/.config/direnv/direnvrc
exists and defines
.B use_runtimes
\(em hard; the symlink deployment of that file is already covered by the
symlink walk.
.TP
.I ~/bin/runtimes
exists and is executable \(em hard; the executable bit lives on the
repository file, tested through the deployed symlink.
.TP
.B uv
and
.B fnm
on
.B PATH
\(em soft
.RB ( "not found (optional)" ):
best-effort installs; the BSD tier legitimately ships without them.
.TP
sdkman \(em soft: installed when either
.B sdk
is on
.B PATH
or
.I ~/.sdkman/
exists (the installer's
.B sdk
function is only loaded in interactive shells, so the install directory is
the reliable presence signal).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress per-check output: the
.B ==>
section banners, the green pass and red fail marks, and the
.B "(no .age files found)"
note. The empty line and final summary line are still printed, counters are
still accumulated, and the exit status is unaffected.
.B \-\-quiet
is only recognized as the first argument; every other argument is ignored.
There is no
.B \-\-help
option.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Base directory for all deployed targets. The symlink checks compare against
.IR "$HOME/<relative path>" ,
and the permissions and secrets checks inspect
.I ~/.ssh
and
.IR ~/.config/tokens .
.TP
.B REPO_DIR
Set by the script to the repository root (the parent of the directory
containing the script) and exported to child processes.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched when checking that
.IR age ,
.IR stow ,
and
.B git
are installed, and for the soft
.BR direnv ,
.BR uv ,
and
.B fnm
presence checks.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every check passed.
.TP
.B 1
At least one check failed. A non-zero status can also result from the script
aborting under
.BR "set \-e" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I scripts/verify.sh
The script itself; its location determines the repository root.
.TP
.IR common/*/ " and " <platform>/*/
Stow packages whose contents are checked against
.RI HOME .
.TP
.I secrets/*/*.age
Encrypted secrets checked for a decrypted counterpart.
.TP
.I ~/.bashrc
Checked for the direnv hook, ordered after the trueline source.
.TP
.I common/direnv/.config/direnv/direnvrc
Content-checked for the
.B use_runtimes
helper.
.TP
.I ~/bin/runtimes
Checked for existence and the executable bit.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh
Checked for mode 700, and for private keys matching
.B id_*
(not
.BR *.pub )
with mode 600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tokens
Target directory for secrets stored under
.IR secrets/tokens/ .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Normal output is a
.B ==>
banner for each section followed by indented lines, each prefixed with a
green check mark for a pass or a red cross for a failure, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
==> Symlinks: common
  <pass> common/bash: ~/.bashrc
  <fail> common/tmux: ~/.tmux.conf missing
.fi
.RE
.PP
(Here
.B <pass>
stands for the green check the script prints and
.B <fail>
for its red cross.) After all sections, an empty line is printed followed by
one of two summary lines:
.B "All N checks passed."
when nothing failed, otherwise
.BI "N failed, M passed."
The summary is printed even under
.BR \-\-quiet .
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the current deployment and print every result:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&./scripts/verify.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run it through
.BR just (1);
the
.B verify
recipe invokes the script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
just verify
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use it as a quiet gate in a script or hook, acting only on the exit status:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&./scripts/verify.sh --quiet || echo "deployment unhealthy"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dots\-setup (1),
.BR dots\-encrypt (1),
.BR dots\-decrypt (1)